From 478742efb938942ab367e1783bf39ad56b4edcec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 12:26:46 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] feat(skippy): describe host-visible KV pages in place

Add skippy_export_kv_page_spans, which describes a sequence's KV page as
spans into the live cells instead of copying it out, and the
SKIPPY_MEMORY_CACHE_HOST_VISIBLE_KV capability bit that gates it.

The bit is a placement fact, not a backend name: the loaded memory sets it
when every K/V/k_idx stream tensor lives in a buffer that reports
ggml_backend_buffer_is_host. Unified-memory backends qualify today; a
discrete backend qualifies the day it allocates the cache in mapped or
managed memory, with no change on the Skippy side. Graph admission still
decides which representations may be cached; the bit is re-applied from
the buffers once anything is admitted.

The copying export and the span export share one walk
(stage_kv_page_walk), so the span order is by construction the payload
byte order skippy_export_kv_page writes. Spans alias the cells and stay
valid until the session decodes, trims below the page, resets, imports
state, or is freed; the caller owns that window.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QA3dWeZ2oS1Nk7FaPpri4e
